{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794909250,"nonce":"8Jblv1wXMo41M8PKGTOLroaPrfk=","message":"2026-11-17 probe on ba823c22 (local changes uncommitted). Changelogs and current tracker comments used; the older HANDOFF was stale.\n\nThe real cn_demo now has a scoped calendar probe: COSTS_CALENDAR=\u003ccycles\u003e, COSTS_CALENDAR_PHASE=rest|pull|turn (default all). COSTS_NO_TRACE=1 disables the diagnostic subscriber for instruction measurements. COSTS_CALENDAR_SNAP=\u003cprefix\u003e is for separate cached/full visual comparisons. Native Metal, headless 900x900 at 1x, simulated 16 ms steps, unpaced: these are per-frame costs, not a browser/window CPU-percentage measurement.\n\nFirst fixed a measurement bug: Headless.apply_queued_updates did not invalidate cached paint after a reactive property write, unlike the windowed runner. A pixel regression failed because the cached rectangle stayed at its old position while a full paint moved it. Matching windowed invalidation makes it pass with CPU rows and the GPU node table. Otherwise a calendar pull could appear cheap by showing stale paint. No Calendar or application-renderer optimization is claimed by this fix.\n\nOptimized release-debug path run, GPU table enabled, three cycles:\n- Rest: 60/60 cached binding_damage/rows frames; render median 0.34 ms.\n- Pull without changing month: 48/48 full paints; render median 3.84 ms, complete headless step 4.84 ms.\n- Month turns: 63/108 full paints, 45 cached rows frames; render median 3.79 ms, complete step p90 24.51 ms.\n\nInstructions retired, no trace/snapshots, identical short pulls: measured 3,200 and 6,400 frames with each table setting and subtracted the shorter process total to reduce startup influence. Each additional frame: CPU rows 60,164,745 instructions; GPU table 60,330,038 (+0.27%, effectively unchanged in this probe). The current GPU-table flag does not defer this work. Peak process footprint varied about 162-271 MiB across those runs; it includes driver/startup peaks and is not evidence of a memory saving or leak.\n\nSample of a separate steady-pull process puts the main work in full-tree paint/collection, LayoutTree::get_absolute_bounds, child traversal, hashing, malloc/free and memmove. Top application leaf was get_absolute_bounds. This is repeated CPU reconstruction and allocation, not merely changing a node row.\n\nDeferral gaps to address:\n1. Calendar page pull is bind_transform_from(offset): PropertyId::Transform updates props, and the window invalidates the whole paint on stateful_prop_update. These reactive transforms are not carried by P2 motion rows. Keep Signal/Computed and the prop-binding API; make the engine carry eligible transform writes, with the current full-paint fallback for unsupported geometry.\n2. Month exit polling queues PropertyId::Region every frame while lingering. That tag requires layout; reconciliation replaces the child list even when the rows still wait. The 13-14 CSS-active full frames per turn line up with that wait. Source finding and a target for a no-op-poll regression, not a measured fix yet.\n3. Browser source gap: WebApp.run_one_frame calls render_tree_with_motion, which passes target_texture=None and try_fast_paint=false. It bypasses the desktop compositor/cache path. Browser adoption and correct invalidation need explicit work; no current-source Chrome performance claim here.\n\nChecks: cached/full images identical at pull, mid-turn and settled (0 pixels differ by \u003e2/255), optimized capture visually reviewed; new cached-transform regression passes with table off/on; four isolated cn_calendar tests pass (continuous swipes, follow/spring back, leave cancellation and instance ownership). Scoped Clippy and formatting pass with existing warnings. No workspace gates, commit, push or issue closure.\n\nEvidence and source hashes: target/calendar-probe/2026-11-17-7936e4b/{metadata.json,blinc-calendar-instructions.json,blinc-calendar-release-paths.log,blinc-calendar-pull.demangled.txt,blinc-calendar-scoped-tests.log,blinc-calendar-release-*.png,source/}. This baseline is ready to compare against the deferral fixes; 70% CPU remains open.","files":null}]}